The proof/trust checks are clean: the kernel reports only Lean’s foundational `propext`, no generated Klean axiom and no `sorryAx`; all four bindings are defined exactly once, the target is unshadowed, and the candidate adds no trust declarations. The remaining—and decisive—check is operational adequacy. I found that the exported `SortVal` includes booleans and floats, while the candidate deliberately treats every selected non-integer sequence as identity. I’m compiling concrete boundary/counterexample theorems and comparing them with the frozen Python operation and supplied K rules.
